WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.600
- So this isn't legal advice,

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.290
but if you're gonna steal
$4 billion worth of Bitcoin,

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.330
maybe keep a low profile
and don't do this.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.770
♪ Everyone worries too
much about what's proper ♪

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:12.742
♪ But not Razz ♪

00:00:12.742 --> 00:00:14.559
♪ No shame, that don't stop her ♪

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:16.800
♪ Blindly following rules is for fools ♪

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.160
- Yes, stealing billions of
dollars in Bitcoin is a crime,

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.430
but those rap videos are
crimes against humanity.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:27.097
(upbeat music)

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.200
Yes, with the development of the internet

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.240
and cryptocurrency, so
too came the opportunity

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.130
for spectacular criming.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:35.370
How spectacular?

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.932
Well, criming in the dollar amount of-

00:00:37.932 --> 00:00:39.179
(dramatic music)

00:00:39.179 --> 00:00:40.810
- Billions.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.647
- Enter one Heather Morgan, 31,

00:00:42.647 --> 00:00:46.900
and her Russian-born husband,
Ilya Dutch Lichtenstein, 34,

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.060
who have been charged by
the Department of Justice

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:53.420
with trying to launder an
astounding $4.5 billion

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:54.870
in stolen Bitcoins.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.560
This case is so captivating,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.430
not just because of the
staggering dollar amounts

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:00.600
or what the DOJ described as the alleged

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.900
labyrinth of cryptocurrency transactions

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.720
to launder the stolen funds,

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.580
but what makes this case
so headline grabbing,

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.860
well, the defendants are, to
put it mildly, a bit out there.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.750
Lichtenstein calls himself
a technology entrepreneur,

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.480
coder, and investor,
while his wife Morgan...

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:21.050
Well, I'll let Morgan introduce
herself in her own words.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:22.458
♪ I'm many things ♪

00:01:22.458 --> 00:01:26.356
♪ A rapper, an economist, a
journalist, a writer, a CEO ♪

00:01:26.356 --> 00:01:28.930
♪ And a dirty, dirty, dirty, dirty ho ♪

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.110
- In addition to being an
irreverent comedic rapper

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.240
using the stage name Razzlekhan,

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.640
yes, I am not making that up,

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.920
Morgan is also self-described
as, "A serial entrepreneur."

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.750
Because of course she is.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:45.190
And a former prolific
contributor to "Forbes" Magazine,

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:48.080
she's even dubbed herself
the Crocodile of Wall Street,

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.500
for some reason.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.870
So today let's think like
a cyber security lawyer

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.910
who may or may not make
investment decisions

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.280
based on dog memes.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.900
So we'll dive into the
stunning indictment,

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.320
examine the characters involved,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.930
and analyze the implications on the law

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:01.580
and cryptocurrency in general,

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.410
and determine if this isn't only

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:06.890
the largest money laundering
scheme in history,

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.300
but also the cringiest.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:10.900
All right, so let's
start with the indictment

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.960
and the weirdos involved.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.537
Back in 2016, hackers
stole 119,754 Bitcoins

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.050
from the Bitfinex cryptocurrency
exchange in Hong Kong.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.060
Since that hack, the value of
the Bitcoins has skyrocketed

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.543
from $72 million to an
astounding $4.5 billion in 2022.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.570
For six years, those monies
successfully evaded the grasp

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:36.940
of law enforcement, until now.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.750
On February 8th, 2022, the
Justice Department announced

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.270
the arrest of Ilya
Lichtenstein and Heather Morgan

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.030
from their New York high rise apartment.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.670
In its statement on the arrest,

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:47.720
the DOJ reported having
successfully recovered

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:52.680
80% of the Bitcoin stolen from
Bitfinex, or $3.6 billion,

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.630
making it the largest
financial seizure in history.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:59.280
Department officials also stated
that they plan to establish

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.610
a court process for victims

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:03.190
seeking to reclaim their stolen Bitcoin.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.700
Lichtenstein and Morgan faced
up to 20 years in prison

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.360
for money laundering charges.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.490
In addition, the pair could
receive an additional sentence

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.660
of up to five years for
the charge of conspiracy

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.070
to defraud the United States.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.000
Now, for those of you who
haven't had money laundering

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.690
explained to them by Saul Goodman-

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.720
- Your filthy drug money
has been transformed

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.690
into nice clean taxable income

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:27.850
brought to you by a savvy
investment in a thriving business.

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:29.870
- It refers to the act of
making large amounts of money

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:31.150
obtained by criminal activity

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.270
appear to come from legal sources.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.050
And conspiracy to
defraud the United States

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:37.360
generally criminalizes
the use of deception

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.270
to cheat the government
out of property or money,

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.410
or to otherwise impair,
obstruct, or defeat

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:43.690
the lawful function of the government.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.880
Interestingly, the
indictment does not allege

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.750
that the pair are the ones
who actually stole the money.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:50.810
It only alleges that an unknown hacker

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:52.510
gave them the stolen Bitcoin,

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:54.180
and that the couple attempted to launder

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.360
the billions of dollars in
crypto to evade law enforcement.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.250
Following their initial court appearance,

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.560
the magistrate judge ruled

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.640
that Lichtenstein could be
released into home detention

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.850
on a $5 million bond
cosigned by his parents.

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:09.260
The bond amount for Morgan
was set at $3 million,

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.810
but both parties were
ordered to remain in custody

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.640
until the bail conditions were met.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.450
Prosecutors opposed granting
the defendants bail,

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.820
claiming that they were
significant flight risks.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.520
Prosecutors argued that just a portion

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.270
of the billions of dollars in crypto

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:24.010
that they've stole or tried to
launder could buy a new house

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:27.320
or, "Buy each of their
parents a private island."

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.060
Prosecutors further
warned that Lichtenstein,

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.060
who holds dual citizenship
in the U.S. and Russia,

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:33.230
was a particular flight risk

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.130
as he could flee to
Russia and seek immunity

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:38.050
if he were no longer held in
the United States' custody.

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:41.750
Federal District Judge Beryl
Howell agreed with prosecutors

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:44.190
and modified the magistrate's
bail order accordingly.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:46.980
On February 14th, the judge
revoked Lichtenstein's bail

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.710
and ordered him to remain
in jail pending trial.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.300
Judge Howell said that
the government had deemed

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:52.700
that Morgan was not as involved

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.630
in planning the alleged
crimes as her husband,

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.180
and that Lichtenstein
was largely in control

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:58.580
of the funds himself.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.650
Judge Howell also considered
Morgan's health issues

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:02.760
as a factor as she recently had surgery

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.300
to remove a lump in her breast.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.310
With follow-up doctor's
appointments expected,

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:08.240
the court released
Morgan into house arrest

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.690
with a GPS ankle monitor.

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:12.000
Now I would be remiss not
to take this opportunity

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.740
to go into the storied background
of Dutch and Razzlekhan.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.170
So before delving into the
specifics of the indictment,

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.060
which I should note are only allegations

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.150
until prove true in a court of law

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:26.090
as the defendants are presumed
innocent until proven guilty,

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:27.930
I wanna give a little
background on the couple

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:29.610
that "The New York Times" has dubbed

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.410
Bitcoin's Bonnie and Clyde.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.690
Now, Lichtenstein, who
goes by the nickname Dutch,

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:35.780
describes himself on his LinkedIn page

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.120
as an angel investor
in technology companies

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.140
and an advisor to various startups.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:41.860
A Twitter account purportedly in his name

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.020
posted frequently on
cryptocurrency trends,

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:46.860
the technology sector,
and the evolution of NFTs.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:48.940
While Dutch seemed to stick

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.730
to being your prototypical crypto bro,

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:53.630
his wife Morgan jumped between being

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:55.650
a self-described serial entrepreneur

00:05:55.650 --> 00:06:00.650
and in her words a, "Raunchy
rapper making surrealist art."

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:04.260
Morgan was also a frequent
contributor to "Forbes" Magazine,

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.630
publishing more than 55 pieces

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:08.960
between July 2017 and September 2021.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.780
Morgan also published articles
on a variety of topics

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:15.650
ranging from soul food
served at the 2021 Met Gala,

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:17.930
why women are better negotiators than men,

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:21.020
and, ironically, expert tips
on protecting your business

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:22.450
from cyber criminals.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:23.320
- It's not ironic.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:24.750
It's just coincidental.

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:28.610
- And, I mean, in fairness,
she probably knows more

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:31.120
about hacking Bitcoin
accounts than most people,

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.160
so maybe she is an expert in this area.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:36.420
Now Morgan is also a
prolific presence on TikTok,

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.400
rapping under the name Razzlekhan.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.647
According to her website,
she's, "Like Genghis Khan,

00:06:41.647 --> 00:06:43.380
"but with more pizazz."

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.090
She also characterizes
herself as basically a mix

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.750
of Hunter S. Thompson and Diane Arbus,

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:50.580
with a sprinkle of Tom Green.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.860
And yeah, I mean I guess she's not

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:54.800
completely wrong about that.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.840
And look, it's easy to make
fun, but all art is subjective

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.890
and it's really not
anyone's place to judge art

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:01.614
as good or bad.

00:07:01.614 --> 00:07:02.485
♪ What ♪

00:07:02.485 --> 00:07:05.984
♪ Razzle Dazzle living in a glass castle ♪

00:07:05.984 --> 00:07:08.960
♪ Don't (beep) with no hassle ♪

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.470
- Okay, I take it back.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:11.690
If the pair is found guilty,

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:13.980
I really hope that the
judge issues an injunction

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.770
preventing Ms. Morgan
from ever rapping again,

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:18.740
and also request that Ms.
Morgan pay restitution

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:21.630
to my eyes and ears for
all the videos I've watched

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:23.930
in preparing for this video.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:25.480
Uh, yeah.

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:28.120
Now let's get onto the complaint itself

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:31.440
and onto the whole issue of
laundering billions of dollars

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:32.940
in stolen cryptocurrency.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:34.630
Now in the statement
announcing the arrest,

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:35.920
here's how the DOJ summarized

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:38.000
the defendants' alleged
money laundering scheme.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.330
The criminal complaint alleges
that Lichtenstein and Morgan

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.550
employed numerous sophisticated
laundering techniques,

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:44.540
including using fictitious identities

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:46.000
to set up online accounts,

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:48.940
utilizing computer programs
to automate the transactions,

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:50.210
a laundering technique that allows

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:51.600
for many transactions to take place

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.040
in a short period of time,

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.850
depositing the stolen funds into accounts

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:57.100
at a variety of virtual currency exchanges

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:58.450
and darknet markets.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:00.520
Basically, they would often
try to convert Bitcoin

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:02.310
into other forms of virtual currency

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.710
and other real tangible assets,

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:07.050
including anonymity
enhanced virtual currency

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:08.700
in a practice known as chain hopping,

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:10.670
and using U.S.-based business accounts

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:12.670
to legitimize their banking activity.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:15.900
According to Jim Lee of the
IRS Criminal Investigation,

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:18.280
they conducted a methodical
and calculated scheme

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:20.990
to allegedly launder and
disguise their vast fortune.

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:23.290
And here's how the government
says that they did it.

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:25.290
In or around August of 2016,

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:27.500
a hacker breached the
Bitfinex security system

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:29.200
and infiltrated its infrastructure.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.130
Once in the network,
the unidentified hacker

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:32.920
was able to initiate over 2,000

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:34.970
unauthorized Bitcoin transactions,

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:39.110
resulting in 119,754
Bitcoins being transferred

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:42.030
from Bitfinex wallets to an
unidentified outside wallet

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:43.960
controlled by the defendants.

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:45.210
Now, if you read the actual complaint

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:46.600
you'd be shocked to see how little

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:48.460
of the billions of dollars in Bitcoin

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:50.140
was actually laundered and spent.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:54.670
In fact, about 80% of the
nearly 120,000 stolen Bitcoin,

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:57.100
the 3.6 billion that the
government recovered,

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:59.580
was found in exactly
the same crypto wallet

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.830
into which they were originally deposited.

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:06.000
As for the remaining 20%, well,
starting in January of 2017,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.660
defendants allegedly began moving

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:10.020
some of the stolen Bitcoins
out of the original wallet

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:12.260
through a series of small
and complex transactions

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:14.610
across multiple accounts and platforms.

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:15.630
Now, one of those accounts

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:17.320
that the Bitcoin was funneled into

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:21.130
was the AlphaBay Marketplace,
a prominent darknet market

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:23.510
that was shut down in 2017 by the FBI

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.110
working in conjunction with
international law enforcement.

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:27.940
Now, the defendants used a high number

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:30.290
of very small transactions
to conceal the path

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:33.330
of the stolen Bitcoin and avoid
law enforcement for years.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:35.490
So the $3.6 billion question is,

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:37.520
how did the defendants get caught?

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:39.920
Well, it was a mix of good
old fashioned police work,

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:41.960
due diligence from the private sector,

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:45.070
and the public nature of
the blockchain itself.

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:48.200
Oh, and the most helpful
element in any investigation,

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:52.100
really stupidly sloppy mistakes
from the alleged criminals.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:54.660
Because on January 31st, 2022,

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.620
law enforcement gained lawful access

00:09:56.620 --> 00:10:00.290
to Dutch's cloud storage
pursuant to a search warrant.

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:02.850
Law enforcement decrypted a single file

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:05.580
in Dutch's cloud storage
that contained a list

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:08.120
of 2,000 virtual currency addresses,

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:10.140
along with the corresponding private keys

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:11.840
to open all of those wallets.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:14.260
That's not surprising because
one of the most tedious things

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:16.350
about cryptocurrency is keeping track

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:18.600
of all your accounts and private keys.

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.560
And that's hard for even people

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:23.250
that have stolen billions
of dollars in crypto coins.

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:25.270
Analyzing the public blockchain records,

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:26.830
the feds confirmed that those addresses

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:28.890
came from the 2016 hack,

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:32.480
because the blockchain
is completely public.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:33.750
Now in the days that followed,

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:35.260
the feds obtained seizure warrants

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:37.920
and used those private keys
contained in Dutch's file

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:40.420
to seize over $3.6 billion in Bitcoin

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:42.330
and place them into the
government's possession.

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.070
Also discovered in Dutch's cloud,

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:47.150
a folder entitled "Personas"
that included biographical info

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:49.220
and documents on numerous individuals,

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:53.470
a file entitled "Passport Ideas"
with links to darknet sites

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:54.920
selling fake passports,

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:57.250
and a folder with potential
financial institutions

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:00.373
to launder the money, which
described AlphaBank as a,

00:11:00.373 --> 00:11:03.940
"Sketchy Russian oligarch bank."

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:06.310
Which is definitely not wrong.

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:08.850
And not only that, but Dutch's
apartment also contained

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:10.950
a bag labeled "Burner phones."

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:13.400
You know, it really makes law
enforcement's job a lot easier

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:15.920
when suspects properly
categorize their crimes

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:18.730
and pre-labeled the
evidence of their arrest.

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:20.460
It's really considerate.

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:22.810
Now the private sector, aided
by government regulation,

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:24.470
was also instrumental in halting

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:26.410
some of the defendants'
illegal activities.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:28.870
Currency exchanges are regulated
by the Treasury Department,

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:30.180
which requires such institutions

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:33.190
to establish anti-money
laundering, AML, programs

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:35.940
and KYC, Know Your Customer, protocols

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:38.730
to make it harder for anonymous
users to transfer money.

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:40.680
In one example of the system working,

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:44.170
the defendants allegedly set
up seven AlphaBay accounts

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:46.180
and six from an established exchange

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:49.710
to hold $186,000 in
Bitcoin under false names.

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:52.400
However, following these
verification protocols,

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:55.310
exchanges were unable to
verify defendants' identities

00:11:55.310 --> 00:11:57.540
or prove that they owned the accounts.

00:11:57.540 --> 00:11:59.750
So the accounts were frozen.

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:01.030
And despite the setbacks,

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:04.020
defendants did successfully
launder some of those Bitcoins

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:05.540
out of third party exchanges.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:06.600
And according to the indictment,

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:08.030
defendants use their ill-gotten gains

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:09.530
to pay for relatively benign items,

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:14.349
such as Uber rides, Hotel.com
stays, a PlayStation, NFTs,

00:12:14.349 --> 00:12:17.310
a $500 Walmart gift card, and even gold.

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:19.390
But the defendants' alleged sloppiness,

00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:23.110
which allegedly bordered on stupidity,

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:25.610
continued to hinder their
efforts at money laundering

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:28.350
while also at the same time
helping law enforcement

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:29.640
to track them down.

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:32.460
The gold that Dutch purchased
with stolen Bitcoin?

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:33.380
Well, he purchased it

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.570
from an online currency exchange account

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:38.520
which he verified with
his real driver's license,

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:41.200
which he sent with a
selfie-style photograph,

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:43.850
and he registered that to
his real email address,

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:47.240
and then Dutch had the gold
shipped to his real home address

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:49.180
at the time in San Francisco.

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:51.730
Then again, I guess this is
exactly what one might expect

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:55.007
from someone who kept
the keys to $3.6 billion

00:12:55.007 --> 00:12:57.640
in cryptocurrency in one cloud file.

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:59.700
Or who married Morgan.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:02.070
Now, Morgan's less-than-genius behavior

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:03.650
also raised some red flags.

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:05.260
In response to one currency exchange

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:07.370
asking follow-up questions
about the origins

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:09.920
of the tens of thousands of
dollars in digital assets

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:10.940
that were to be deposited

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:14.070
into Morgan's newly
created business account,

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:17.154
Morgan gave the following
not at all suspicious answer.

00:13:17.154 --> 00:13:20.157
"My boyfriend, now husband,
gifted me cryptocurrency

00:13:20.157 --> 00:13:24.777
"over several years, 2014,
2015, which have appreciated.

00:13:24.777 --> 00:13:27.100
"I have been keeping
them in cold storage."

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:28.700
That obviously cagey answer

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:30.607
is the money laundering equivalent of,

00:13:30.607 --> 00:13:34.147
"My boyfriend, why he goes to
a different school in Canada.

00:13:34.147 --> 00:13:35.950
"You wouldn't know him."

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:37.060
Another red flag?

00:13:37.060 --> 00:13:38.410
Well, "The Wall Street Journal" reports

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:40.480
that a friend of Ms.
Morgan's noted how she

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:42.190
and her husband's lifestyle did not match

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:43.410
their purported wealth.

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.840
While planning to buy a $2
million condo in New York,

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:47.290
the defendants had a modest wedding

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:49.650
and the couple used airline miles to fly.

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:51.620
You know, just like the rest of us.

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:54.080
Now, maybe the best part
about this entire case

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:57.560
is that whether you a crypto
skeptic or a crypto booster,

00:13:57.560 --> 00:13:59.560
there's plenty of material for both camps.

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:01.320
Crypto critics have often noted

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:04.170
that less than desirable
groups, like white supremacists,

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:06.960
have embraced crypto as a means
of evading law enforcement

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:08.970
and avoiding legal judgements.

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:11.730
Furthermore, in 2021, it
marked an all-time high

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:14.020
of $14 billion in cryptocurrency theft

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:17.150
with the innocent suffering
brunt of the consequences.

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:18.540
And of course the Bitfinex hack

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:20.510
and subsequent money
laundering only reinforces

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:23.090
critics' argument that
cryptocurrency is not just ripe

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:26.010
for criminal conduct, but
is also not nearly as secure

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:28.150
and unhackable as its boosters claim.

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:29.740
But at the same time,
crypto advocates show

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:31.600
that the blockchain's secure technology

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:33.330
is working exactly as intended.

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:34.607
In the words of crypto boosters,

00:14:34.607 --> 00:14:36.587
"It's further proof that
the nature blockchain,

00:14:36.587 --> 00:14:40.277
"and it being always
public, allows investigators

00:14:40.277 --> 00:14:42.937
"to follow the money in ways
that would be impossible

00:14:42.937 --> 00:14:46.347
"in complex webs of shell
companies and cash smuggling

00:14:46.347 --> 00:14:49.400
"that exist with normal financial tools."

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:51.300
Crypto advocates argue
that the public nature

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:54.480
of blockchain technology not
only serves as self-regulating,

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:58.030
an anti-fraud measure, but that
the global blockchain ledger

00:14:58.030 --> 00:15:00.670
allows police to track down
the stolen cryptocurrency

00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:02.897
in ways that would not be
possible with fiat money.

00:15:02.897 --> 00:15:05.160
"Bloomberg" columnist
Matt Levine highlights

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:08.040
this inherent contradiction
perfectly, as usual,

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:10.727
saying, "You might think that
a federal criminal indictment

00:15:10.727 --> 00:15:13.737
"for $4.5 billion of
Bitcoin money laundering

00:15:13.737 --> 00:15:15.047
"would be vindication

00:15:15.047 --> 00:15:17.717
"of the 'Bitcoin is for
money launderers' side.

00:15:17.717 --> 00:15:19.957
"But I want to tell you, no, it is not.

00:15:19.957 --> 00:15:21.830
"What allegedly happened
here is that hackers stole

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:24.927
"4.5 billion of Bitcoin
from a crypto exchange,

00:15:24.927 --> 00:15:26.817
"and stealing from exchanges absolutely

00:15:26.817 --> 00:15:28.567
"is a major function of crypto,

00:15:28.567 --> 00:15:31.307
"and then they had a
horrible time laundering it.

00:15:31.307 --> 00:15:33.517
"They managed to extract only
a relatively small portion

00:15:33.517 --> 00:15:35.007
"of the money for actual spending,

00:15:35.007 --> 00:15:36.857
"and each time they got money out

00:15:36.857 --> 00:15:39.217
"the feds were able to
trace it from the hack

00:15:39.217 --> 00:15:42.070
"all the way to legitimate
accounts with their names on it."

00:15:42.070 --> 00:15:43.807
Matt Levine goes on to analogize,

00:15:43.807 --> 00:15:45.493
"If you rob a bank and
steal a sack of money

00:15:45.493 --> 00:15:47.097
"and the bills are sequentially numbered

00:15:47.097 --> 00:15:48.687
"and a dye pack explodes in the sack

00:15:48.687 --> 00:15:50.417
"and you drive directly to another bank

00:15:50.417 --> 00:15:52.707
"and hand them a dye-stain sack and say,

00:15:52.707 --> 00:15:54.637
"'I'd like to make a deposit, please,'

00:15:54.637 --> 00:15:55.987
"you will totally get arrested

00:15:55.987 --> 00:15:58.257
"and you will probably get
charged with money laundering.

00:15:58.257 --> 00:16:00.807
"But in no meaningful sense
did you launder the money.

00:16:00.807 --> 00:16:02.447
"It still has the dye on it.

00:16:02.447 --> 00:16:03.920
"That's what happened here."

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:05.917
And as another crypto expert said,

00:16:05.917 --> 00:16:07.647
"The problem with
laundering cryptocurrency

00:16:07.647 --> 00:16:09.947
"is that if you make a
mistake five years ago,

00:16:09.947 --> 00:16:12.480
"that's still on the blockchain
for everyone to see."

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:14.060
But I guess one thing
that we can all agree

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:16.460
is that whatever your
take on cryptocurrency,

00:16:16.460 --> 00:16:18.530
remember that talking about it constantly

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:20.650
does not make you cool.

00:16:20.650 --> 00:16:22.390
Now, admittedly, we've all had our fun,

00:16:22.390 --> 00:16:24.360
